  <section>
    <h2>Why battery storage matters for fire safety</h2>
    <p>Lithium-ion batteries (in phones, laptops, tablets, power banks) can enter 'thermal runaway' — an internal chemical reaction that generates its own heat, which triggers more reaction, which generates more heat. Once thermal runaway starts, the battery can reach several hundred degrees and ignite nearby materials.</p>
    <p>Triggers for thermal runaway: physical damage (puncture, crush, bend), short circuit (metal object across the terminals), extreme heat (leaving in direct sun, near a heat source), or internal defect (which is what causes swelling — internal degradation).</p>
    <p>Once a lithium-ion battery is swollen, it has an ongoing internal issue. Not every swollen battery will catch fire, but the probability increases if it's stored badly (near flammable material, in heat, next to metal).</p>
    <p>The whole point of safe storage rules is to reduce trigger conditions during the days between deciding to recycle and pickup happening. Simple containment is usually sufficient.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to do with a swollen phone or laptop battery specifically</h2>
    <p>This is the most common damaged-battery scenario in Kochi households. Correct response:</p>
    <p>Do: leave the battery inside the phone / laptop (do not try to remove); place the device on a non-flammable surface (ceramic tile, glass, concrete); keep away from direct sunlight, heat, and other metals; place away from anything flammable (paper, carpet, curtains, wooden furniture); take a photo; book pickup within a few days.</p>
    <p>Don't: puncture the battery with a screwdriver or knife; crush the phone / laptop trying to make it flat again; bend the device; submerge in water; put in a metal container (which could short across the terminals); throw in regular trash; leave in a hot vehicle boot.</p>
    <p>If the phone / laptop is actively hot to touch or making unusual noises, move it outdoors to a non-flammable surface (concrete driveway, tile patio) and message the team urgently.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Leaking UPS or inverter battery — different scenario</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid batteries (UPS, inverter, car batteries) don't have the same thermal runaway risk as lithium-ion, but they have their own hazards — the acid inside is corrosive and the battery contents are toxic. Correct response for a leaking lead-acid battery:</p>
    <p>Use gloves (rubber or nitrile) if you need to move the battery. Skin contact with battery acid should be washed off with water for 15+ minutes.</p>
    <p>Place on a non-porous surface (ceramic tile, concrete). Not on carpet, wood, or absorbent surfaces that would soak up the acid.</p>
    <p>Ventilate the area — battery leakage can produce hydrogen gas.</p>
    <p>Contain any leaked acid with sand, kitty litter, or similar absorbent material (do not use water, which spreads the leak). Cardboard or newspaper works in a pinch.</p>
    <p>Book pickup quickly and flag 'leaking UPS/inverter battery' with a photo so the team brings appropriate handling equipment.</p>
  </section>

